  values ($mw$victor-tip-chart-acetylene$mw$, $mw$Victor Acetylene Tip Chart: Cutting (1-101 / 3-101), Welding (W-J / 100 series) and MFA Heating Tips$mw$, $mw$The Victor acetylene pressure card reproduced in full: tip size vs metal thickness with cutting-oxygen, preheat-oxygen and acetylene pressures, cutting speed and kerf; welding nozzle flow data with drill sizes; and MFA rosebud heating-tip pressures.$mw$, $mw$All pressures are **psig at the regulator with the torch flowing**. Victor measured cutting-tip values through a 25 ft × 3/8" hose for tip 6 and larger. Torches fitted with flashback arrestors can need **up to 25% more pressure** as tip size goes up, but acetylene still tops out at **15 psig**.

## Cutting tips - Series 1-101 and 3-101 (acetylene)

| Metal thickness | Tip size | Cutting oxygen (psig) | Preheat oxygen (psig)* | Acetylene (psig) | Speed (ipm) | Kerf width (in) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1/8" | 000 | 20-25 | 3-5 | 3-5 | 20-30 | .04 |
| 1/4" | 00 | 20-25 | 3-5 | 3-5 | 27-30 | .05 |
| 3/8" | 0 | 25-30 | 3-5 | 3-5 | 24-28 | .06 |
| 1/2" | 0 | 30-35 | 3-6 | 3-5 | 20-24 | .06 |
| 3/4" | 1 | 30-35 | 4-7 | 3-5 | 17-21 | .07 |
| 1" | 2 | 35-40 | 4-8 | 3-6 | 15-19 | .09 |
| 2" | 3 | 40-45 | 5-10 | 4-8 | 12-15 | .11 |
| 3" | 4 | 40-50 | 5-10 | 5-11 | 9-12 | .12 |
| 4" | 5 | 45-55 | 6-12 | 6-13 | 8-11 | .15 |
| 6" | 6** | 45-55 | 6-15 | 8-14 | 6-8 | .15 |
| 10" | 7** | 45-55 | 6-20 | 10-15 | 4-5 | .34 |
| 12" | 8** | 45-55 | 7-25 | 10-15 | 3-5 | .41 |

\* Preheat-oxygen column applies to **3-hose machine torches only**. On a 2-hose hand torch the preheat pressure is set by the cutting-oxygen setting.
\*\* Tip 6 and larger: use a heavy-duty torch and 3/8" hose.

**Caution (from the card):** never draw more than 1/7 of an acetylene cylinder's contents per hour. If you need more flow, manifold cylinders.

### Reading a Victor cutting-tip number

`1-1-101` = tip size **1**, fuel type **1** (acetylene), series **101** (standard hand-cutting). A `0-3-101` is size 0 in the 3-101 series (same chart, different seat length for other torches). GPN/HPN series tips are for **propane/natural gas** and use a different chart; do not use them on acetylene.

## Welding tips - W-J / 100 series (Welding Nozzle Flow Data)

| Metal thickness | Tip size | Drill size (orifice) | Oxygen min-max (psig) | Acetylene min-max (psig) | Acetylene consumption (scfh) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| up to 1/32" | 000 | 75 (.022) | 3-5 | 3-5 | 1-2 |
| 1/64" - 3/64" | 00 | 70 (.028) | 3-5 | 3-5 | 1.5-3 |
| 1/32" - 5/64" | 0 | 65 (.035) | 3-5 | 3-5 | 2-4 |
| 3/64" - 3/32" | 1 | 60 (.040) | 3-5 | 3-5 | 3-6 |
| 1/16" - 1/8" | 2 | 56 (.046) | 3-5 | 3-5 | 5-10 |
| 1/8" - 3/16" | 3 | 53 (.060) | 4-7 | 3-6 | 8-18 |
| 3/16" - 1/4" | 4 | 49 (.073) | 5-10 | 4-7 | 10-25 |
| 1/4" - 1/2" | 5 | 43 (.089) | 6-12 | 5-8 | 15-35 |
| 1/2" - 3/4" | 6 | 36 (.106) | 7-14 | 6-9 | 25-45 |
| 3/4" - 1 1/4" | 7 | 30 (.128) | 8-16 | 8-10 | 30-60 |
| 1 1/4" - 2" | 8 | 29 (.136) | 10-19 | 9-12 | 35-75 |
| 2 1/2" - 3" | 10 | 27 (.144) | 12-24 | 12-15 | 50-100 |
| 3 1/2" - 4" | 12* | 25 (.149) | 18-28 | 12-15 | 80-160 |

\* Tip 12 requires the HD310C torch and 3/8" hose.

The drill size is handy for checking a tip: if a #60 drill shank slides into a size-1 tip freely the orifice is worn oversize and the tip should be replaced.

## MFA heating nozzles (rosebuds)

| Tip size | Acetylene (psig) | Oxygen (psig) | Acetylene (scfh) min-max | Oxygen (scfh) min-max |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 4 | 6-10 | 8-12 | 6-20 | 7-22 |
| 6 | 8-12 | 10-15 | 14-40 | 15-44 |
| 8 | 10-15 | 20-30 | 30-80 | 33-88 |
| 10 | 12-15 | 30-40 | 40-100 | 44-110 |
| 12* | 12-15 | 50-60 | 60-150 | 66-165 |
| 15* | 12-15 | 50-60 | 90-220 | 99-244 |

\* Sizes 12 and 15 need the HD310C torch and 3/8" hose. Acetylene has roughly 1 470 gross BTU per cubic foot. A size 8 rosebud pulling 80 scfh needs at least **560 ft³ of acetylene cylinder capacity** (80 × 7) to respect the 1/7 rule, which usually means a manifold of two or more #4 (approx. 300 ft³) cylinders.

## Notes for students

- Pressures on the card are **starting points**. Adjust for a clean neutral flame and a clean cut; do not chase the numbers.
- When the shop torch has a **flashback arrestor** on the handle, set toward the top of each range.
- Keep the printed Victor card in your toolbox. Every school and every Victor dealer hands out the same one.

## Related

- [Setting up, lighting and shutting down a Victor oxy-acetylene torch](/article/oxy-acetylene-setup-victor)
